president biden is imposing new  sanctions on the cuban regime after the  violent crackdown on anti-government  protesters earlier this month  the move comes as some boaters in  florida plan to launch a flotilla of  private vessels  toward cuba this morning manuela jorquez  is in miami  manny good morning what are they  planning exactly  good morning their plan to have dozens  of boaters join in haven't quite  materialized at least not just yet but  the vote  voters who are here at this marina say  they plan to head to key west check in  with the coast guard and then sail  towards cuba where they will show their  support with fireworks and  chinese lanterns many have shown their  support for protesters in cuba on the  waters off miami  but now organizers of the flotilla say  they'll head to 15 nautical miles from  havana  which would be international waters as  they make a show of support for the  cuban people  captain adam shammy with the u.s coast  guard says it's legal  but crossing into cuban waters is not  and can incur fines and prison time  there's also the weather what might seem  like it's pretty safe waters just a few  miles from shore  when you're 50 miles from shore can look  very differently  the planned trip comes as the biden  administration announced  new sanctions on alvaro lopez miera the  cuban minister of defense  as well as the cuban interior ministry  special brigade  for repressing the pro-democracy  protests in cuba that began july 11th  there are reports of possibly hundreds  detained since the protests  in a statement president biden said the  cuban people have the same right to  freedom of expression  and peaceful assembly as all people  miami mayor francis juarez argues cuba  is not a priority of the biden  administration the cuban people  are defending themselves with rocks and  sticks against  guns and clubs and so i've even called  for things as dramatic as  not taking off the table the possibility  of some level of military intervention  jorge duani is the director of the cuban  research institute at florida  international university  he says the biden administration is  trying to find the middle ground between  the more open policies of the obama  administration  and former president trump's swift  reversal much of the calculus  regarding what to do next with cuba has  to do with  next year's midterm election here in  florida and i'm sure that's part of the  discussion  within the white house  duwani also says the biden  administration stated goal of restoring  internet service to the island which was  cut off by the communist regime  after the uprisings there may be the  best way to help protesters keep the  momentum  going gail all right manny thank you  very much
 

